Harry Houdini is widely regarded as one  of the greatest magicians escape artists  and showman of all time however he was  actually much more than this he can even  be considered a kind of polymath he was  a prolific writer he had a patent for a  diving suit he invented he was an  aviator and he was a Hollywood film star  for a time but perhaps what's most  interesting about Houdini is that many  of his tricks weren't in fact tricks at  all but rather simply examples of  incredible mastery over his own body he  may have had more in common with the  strongmen of his era such as the mighty  Adam or the inventor of muscle control  max ik either way he was a true legend  and in this video we're going to explore  a little bit about his life and also  what we can learn from his training and  how he can develop that same kind of  mastery  so Houdini was actually born Erik vice  in Budapest in 1874 he is one of seven  siblings and one of his brothers  actually also went on to become a  well-known escape artist he moved to the  US with his family in 1879 and there he  would change his name to Erik Vice the  German pronunciation although I may well  be saying that completely wrong as usual  in his youth air which was already  highly athletic he was a successful  runner a boxer and a gymnast at the age  of nine he became a trapeze artist known  as Erik Prince of air who would continue  practicing boxing gymnastics and  jiu-jitsu well into his adulthood Erik  also discovered his passion for magic in  his youth he was inspired by a fellow  magician who Dan whose name he borrowed  for his own act he falsely believed that  calling himself Houdini would translate  in French - like who Dan later on in his  life however he go on to become highly  critical of who Dan the name Harry might  well have come from Harry Keller who was  also a hero of his or it might have just  been an adaptation of Eric which becomes  arrey arrey Harry you get it as a  magician Harry got off to a somewhat  rough start to begin with his acts  focused mostly on card tricks and he  called himself the king of cards Yelp's  he already had a flair for the dramatic  it wasn't until he challenged a police  officer telling him that he could escape  from his handcuffs they would attract  the attention of the media that  obviously captured the public's  imagination and so for a while escaping  from handcuffs would become his bread  and butter he even called himself Harry  king of Han  cuffs it wasn't long however until he  started to develop his accent it became  much more dramatic and dangerous some of  his most famous acts included escaping  from milk cans water torture  straitjackets while suspended in midair  jail cells uniquely designed handcuffs  created especially to thwart him by  master locksmiths being buried alive  underwater coffins and even the stomach  of a beached whale or sea monster other  displays included to him materializing  through walls swallowing lots of needles  and then regurgitating them later on  threaded and even taking punches to the  stomach he'd also jump off of bridges  into rivers from great heights but the  real question that I'm interested in is  how how does Harry achieve all this and  what can we learn from Harry Houdini's  training well it's only right to point  out that some of Harry's tricks were  indeed just that tricks or illusions  they might incorporate trapdoors for  instance he was also a keen businessman  and not above occasionally  anti-competitive measures however we  shouldn't overlook that many of his acts  were also fully real and examples of  incredible dexterity strength and  bravery it's well known for instance I  collected hundreds upon hundreds of  locks and shackles and chains would  practice unlocking them breaking out of  them sometimes he'd quickly open them  using concealed picks other times he'd  apply force in the correct manner to  break them open his knowledge of locks  was second to none at one point he was  even considering becoming a locksmith  himself and is even enlisted by the US  Army to help train the troops so they  could escape capture as well Houdini  practice with single-minded  determination the same kind of absolute  dedication and passion that we've seen  with several other people I've talked  about on this channel like Bruce Lee or  da Vinci he was constantly practicing  like wim HOF many years later  Houdini's training also involved  submerging himself into ice-cold water  in a bathtub that he kept in his home  this would shock his nervous system and  he then practiced bringing it back under  conscious control he'd also practice  holding his breath in here and reports  vary on precisely how long he could hold  his breath for but the best estimate  seems to be three minutes forty seconds  of course right now that's nothing  spectacular some free divers can hold  their breath for 10 15 even 20 minutes  David Blaine managed to hold his breath  for an impressive  ten minutes on the stage but at the time  it was extremely impressive and it  wasn't his only feat of breath control  another act he was sealed inside an  airtight coffin underwater for 90  minutes and used very shallow breaths in  order to keep himself calm and not use  up all the oxygen he's talked in the  past about the importance of keeping  calm and focused even in  life-threatening situations and it's  highly likely that he had some kind of  meditation or practice breathing as part  of his training this is all something  that we'll be diving into in more depth  and future no pun intended in short it  seems that Houdini had the uncanny  ability to control aspects of his  autonomic nervous system consciously is  something only a few other individuals  have demonstrated the ability to do  since Houdini was generally fascinated  with the human body and uncovering his  notes we see lots of sketches of anatomy  and musculature in one sketch from his  notes we see the spine split into the  five sections demonstrating mobility and  flexibility another aspect of Houdini's  training was training his internal  organs in order to be able to perform  that regurgitation trick he would  practice swallowing objects tied to  string then going about his business as  normal  before regurgitating them whole much  later on again this seems to show that  with practice you can gain great control  even over the muscles in your stomach  it's maybe possible due to motor neuron  plasticity the branching of nerves and  the creation of new asset I'll :  receptors he also developed his  dexterity to an incredible degree he was  likely ambidextrous purely through  practice and he could control his feet  with great dexterity to reportedly his  training involved tying and untying  knots repeatedly with his toes and it's  been said that he achieved the same kind  of dexterity in his toes that the  average person would have in their  fingers control of the feet hands even  organs an autonomic response can  potentially be seen as extensions of  muscle control this is a concept that  was big during Houdini's heyday and that  was employed by many strongmen in order  to accomplish their amazing  demonstrations of raw power if he  followed the bio near then you'll know  what muscle control is it basically  means the ability to recruit more motor  units not only within the muscle that  you can already consciously control but  also within smaller and more obscure  muscles someone with great muscle  control might be able to flex a lat or  trap without contracting the surrounding  muscle and reports seem to support that  Houdini was into this kind of thing they  recount his ability to make muscles pop  up and contract  in his hand in his forearms even his  little finger and it's been suggested  that Houdini actually learned the  ability to contract his stomach and take  punches from the mighty Adam this would  come in handy when his escape and cuffs  or needs escaping straitjackets the way  he would 